# Train Dispatch Engine Rules

The 60fps rAF loop is the perf-critical path. Get this wrong and the game lags.

## State (CRITICAL)
- NEVER put the `trains` array or per-frame positions in `useState`. Store ALL mutable
  game state (trains, switches, positions, tick) in `useRef`.
- Only `useState` low-frequency, render-triggering values: `score`, `lives`, `level`, `streak`.
- Re-render the Konva layer each frame with a lightweight `forceUpdate` (bump a counter),
  not by setting the trains array into state.

```ts
// BAD
const [trains, setTrains] = useState<Train[]>([]); // rAF + setState = lag

// GOOD
const trainsRef = useRef<Train[]>([]);
const [, forceUpdate] = useReducer((n) => n + 1, 0);
// in rAF tick: mutate trainsRef.current, then forceUpdate()
```

## Loop lifecycle
- Start `requestAnimationFrame` in `useEffect` on mount; store id in a `useRef`.
- ALWAYS `cancelAnimationFrame` in the effect cleanup. No leaked loops.
- Hook returns `{ trains, switches, score, lives, level, toggleSwitch(id) }`.

## Movement & collision
- Track segments = arrays of `{x, y}` waypoints. Interpolate with `lerp()`.
- Advance to next segment when distance to next waypoint `< 2px`.
- At a switch, read switch state (`straight | divert`) to pick the next segment.
- CRASH when two trains are `< 20px` apart on the same segment, or a train hits the wrong platform.

## Konva touch (mobile)
- Stage must resize with container via `ResizeObserver`; `Stage width={containerWidth}`.
- Switch hit areas min 44px: wrap the visible `Circle` with a transparent `Rect` or use `hitStrokeWidth`.
- Use `onTouchStart` on switches, not `onClick` alone — canvas touch != mouse.
